"comparison": "Both NVDA and AMD disclose risks related to their reliance on third-party providers for critical operational functions, though the nature of the reliance differs. NVDA focuses on the ecosystem dependency for software and content (developing applications and providing content for its platforms like GeForce NOW), warning that a failure by these third parties to build or maintain these assets could adversely affect customer demand. In contrast, AMD highlights its reliance on third parties for physical supply-chain logistics (product distribution, transportation) and IT services (data center operations, help desk). AMD warns that if these providers fail to fulfill responsibilities timely or if contracts are not renewed, internal operations and product distribution could be materially adversely affected. While both address 'reliance on third parties,' NVDA's risk is centered on the viability and availability of digital content/application ecosystems driving demand, whereas AMD's risk is centered on the logistical and technical infrastructure execution required to distribute products and maintain internal operations.",
